1
00:00:07,799 --> 00:00:13,531
He took Evelyn Marsh down on the first of April,
which she would later think was a joke,

2
00:00:13,551 --> 00:00:14,708
and which was not.

3
00:00:15,278 --> 00:00:20,840
She had asked him, in the week after the bell, what
came next. She had asked it the way

4
00:00:20,860 --> 00:00:26,872
she asked everything now — quietly, directly, at an hour
when nobody else was in the building.

5
00:00:26,892 --> 00:00:32,648
And he had looked at her for a long moment and said:
come to the old building at midnight.

6
00:00:32,668 --> 00:00:33,675
Wear flat shoes.

7
00:00:34,244 --> 00:00:39,914
The freight elevator went down past the basement. She
felt it in her stomach first — the

8
00:00:39,934 --> 00:00:44,440
drop that went on too long — and then in her
ears, which popped twice.

9
00:00:45,131 --> 00:00:46,250
How deep is this?

10
00:00:46,771 --> 00:00:48,190
Deeper than the ground is.

11
00:00:48,710 --> 00:00:49,850
That isn't an answer.

12
00:00:50,369 --> 00:00:51,988
It's the only accurate one.

13
00:00:53,509 --> 00:00:58,890
The doors opened onto white, and Evelyn Marsh
stepped out of the elevator into the rest

14
00:00:58,910 --> 00:00:59,634
of her life.

15
00:01:00,204 --> 00:01:05,574
She did not say anything for a long time. She
walked. She put her hand on the glass of

16
00:01:05,594 --> 00:01:10,902
the first laboratory, and then the second, and then
she stopped in front of a rack of

17
00:01:10,922 --> 00:01:16,480
positive-pressure suits and touched the sleeve of
one, the way you might touch the sleeve

18
00:01:16,500 --> 00:01:17,670
of a wedding dress.

19
00:01:18,361 --> 00:01:19,526
This is grade four.

20
00:01:20,046 --> 00:01:20,686
Yes.

21
00:01:21,206 --> 00:01:26,678
There are six grade-four labs in this country outside
the government. I've read the inspection

22
00:01:26,698 --> 00:01:29,482
reports for all of them. This
isn't one of them.

23
00:01:30,003 --> 00:01:30,623
No.

24
00:01:31,143 --> 00:01:31,928
Who built it?

25
00:01:33,248 --> 00:01:33,953
I did.

26
00:01:35,673 --> 00:01:41,424
She did not ask how. That was the thing he would
remember. Nadia would have asked how.

27
00:01:41,444 --> 00:01:47,129
Catherine would have asked how much. Evelyn simply
accepted it, the way a person in a

28
00:01:47,149 --> 00:01:52,834
dream accepts that the house has more rooms than it
did yesterday, and asked the only

29
00:01:52,854 --> 00:01:54,512
question she cared about.

30
00:01:55,201 --> 00:01:55,971
What's it for?

31
00:01:57,990 --> 00:02:00,070
He showed her the cold room first.

32
00:02:00,640 --> 00:02:06,361
The grey machine had been stripped of its disguise
when it came down. Gus had removed

33
00:02:06,381 --> 00:02:12,440
the ducting and the false panels and the service plate
that said Arcturus Climate Systems,

34
00:02:12,460 --> 00:02:18,518
and what was left was what it had always been: a
cryogenic cradle, nine feet tall, frosted

35
00:02:18,538 --> 00:02:21,760
white, with a viewing window the
size of a door.

36
00:02:22,330 --> 00:02:28,082
He touched a panel. The frost on the window cleared,
from the centre outwards, like breath

37
00:02:28,102 --> 00:02:29,428
fading from a mirror.

38
00:02:29,998 --> 00:02:33,956
Inside, in a pale mist, suspended
upright, a figure slept.

39
00:02:34,526 --> 00:02:40,976
It was nine feet tall. It was grey — the grey of wet
stone, or a winter sky — and hairless,

40
00:02:40,996 --> 00:02:47,089
and its body was built along the lines of a man, but
a man designed by someone who had

41
00:02:47,109 --> 00:02:53,203
found the original too fragile. Its shoulders were
too wide. Its hands were too large.

42
00:02:53,223 --> 00:02:59,530
Its chest rose and fell once every six seconds, in
time with the compressor, in time with

43
00:02:59,550 --> 00:03:05,643
the breath the building had been breathing for
eighteen months. Its face was calm, and

44
00:03:05,663 --> 00:03:11,686
blank, and almost beautiful, the way a statue's
face is beautiful, because nothing is

45
00:03:11,706 --> 00:03:12,396
behind it.

46
00:03:14,467 --> 00:03:20,519
Most people, the Director had calculated, would
scream. Some would run. A few would simply

47
00:03:20,539 --> 00:03:20,924
faint.

48
00:03:21,493 --> 00:03:23,975
Evelyn Marsh stepped closer to the glass.

49
00:03:24,665 --> 00:03:25,584
It's beautiful.

50
00:03:27,104 --> 00:03:32,628
Behind her, in the quiet of one skull, the Wesker
half of the mind made a small note.

51
00:03:32,648 --> 00:03:38,301
And the Adolf half — for the first time since they
had met her — looked at Evelyn Marsh

52
00:03:38,321 --> 00:03:40,127
with something like respect.

53
00:03:40,818 --> 00:03:43,963
That one is not afraid of the future.

54
00:03:44,484 --> 00:03:47,596
No. She's in love with it. That's
much more dangerous.

55
00:03:49,316 --> 00:03:49,969
What is it?

56
00:03:50,490 --> 00:03:52,730
A Tyrant. Series T-zero-zero.

57
00:03:53,250 --> 00:03:55,459
A Tyrant. Is it — was it — a person?

58
00:03:55,979 --> 00:03:58,943
It was never born. It was never
anything but this.

59
00:03:59,463 --> 00:04:00,480
Then what is it for?

60
00:04:01,800 --> 00:04:04,333
That depends on what we decide
the world is for.

61
00:04:06,354 --> 00:04:07,829
Then he showed her the blood.

62
00:04:08,397 --> 00:04:14,160
He drew it himself, at a bench in the second
laboratory, without a tourniquet, because

63
00:04:14,180 --> 00:04:20,413
his veins did not need one. He put a drop under a
microscope that cost more than her parents'

64
00:04:20,433 --> 00:04:25,658
house, and put the image up on a screen the size
of a door, and let her watch.

65
00:04:26,228 --> 00:04:31,577
She watched for seven minutes without speaking. She had
spent her twenties studying viruses,

66
00:04:31,597 --> 00:04:36,830
and she knew within the first minute that she was
looking at something no human laboratory

67
00:04:36,850 --> 00:04:42,083
had made. By the fifth minute she understood that it
was not an infection — that the cells

68
00:04:42,103 --> 00:04:47,219
it moved through were not sick, but improved. By the
tenth, she had worked out, with the

69
00:04:47,239 --> 00:04:52,064
terrible quickness that was the whole of her
talent, where the blood had come from.

70
00:04:52,754 --> 00:04:54,075
This is Control Seven.

71
00:04:54,595 --> 00:04:55,235
Yes.

72
00:04:55,754 --> 00:04:59,298
Howard's calibration sample. The one
that broke the analyzer.

73
00:04:59,817 --> 00:05:00,458
Yes.

74
00:05:01,778 --> 00:05:02,526
It's yours.

75
00:05:04,045 --> 00:05:05,658
He took off his glasses.

76
00:05:06,228 --> 00:05:12,232
Howard Lessing had seen those eyes and been sick in
a parking lot. Evelyn Marsh looked

77
00:05:12,252 --> 00:05:18,257
into them from two feet away, under the cold light
of a laboratory that did not exist,

78
00:05:18,277 --> 00:05:23,232
and — the Cold-Read told him, with total
clarity — felt no fear at all.

79
00:05:23,802 --> 00:05:29,511
What she felt was vindication. The overwhelming,
trembling vindication of a young woman

80
00:05:29,531 --> 00:05:35,110
who had always believed, with no evidence, that the
world was stranger and larger and

81
00:05:35,130 --> 00:05:40,971
more magnificent than the people around her would
admit, and who had just been shown that

82
00:05:40,991 --> 00:05:41,894
she was right.

83
00:05:42,584 --> 00:05:43,410
What are you?

84
00:05:43,930 --> 00:05:44,820
The first one.

85
00:05:45,339 --> 00:05:46,228
The first what?

86
00:05:46,747 --> 00:05:48,449
That's what you're going to find out.

87
00:05:50,468 --> 00:05:53,078
He gave her the Arklay
sublevel that night.

88
00:05:53,648 --> 00:05:59,256
Not all of it — the cold room and its sleeper would
remain his. But the laboratories,

89
00:05:59,276 --> 00:06:04,885
the equipment, the budget, and a programme with a
name. He had chosen the name before

90
00:06:04,905 --> 00:06:10,513
she ever came down, from a sealed black card in the
air that only he could see, and a

91
00:06:10,533 --> 00:06:13,560
word that the System had told
him was not yet.

92
00:06:14,250 --> 00:06:20,226
It's called the Progenitor Initiative. You'll run
it. You'll report to me and to no one

93
00:06:20,246 --> 00:06:24,086
else. Not Catherine. Not the board.
Not the FDA. Nobody.

94
00:06:24,607 --> 00:06:25,504
What's the work?

95
00:06:26,024 --> 00:06:30,804
Understanding what's in my blood. And then —
learning to make more of it. Safely.

96
00:06:31,324 --> 00:06:32,281
And the — the Tyrant?

97
00:06:32,800 --> 00:06:34,870
Understanding what's in its blood, too.

98
00:06:36,389 --> 00:06:37,394
And if I say no?

99
00:06:37,913 --> 00:06:42,815
Then you go back up in the elevator, and you run the
assay programme for the rest of your

100
00:06:42,835 --> 00:06:47,682
life, and you're very successful and very well paid,
and you never come down here again,

101
00:06:47,702 --> 00:06:52,051
and you spend every night of the rest of your
life knowing that you could have.

102
00:06:53,771 --> 00:06:59,215
She did not say no. She had never been going to. He
had known that since the night at

103
00:06:59,235 --> 00:06:59,858
the piano.

104
00:07:00,427 --> 00:07:06,450
But she took a long time to say yes, standing in the
middle of the white corridor with

105
00:07:06,470 --> 00:07:12,492
her hands open at her sides, like a woman in a
cathedral. And when she did, it did not

106
00:07:12,512 --> 00:07:15,022
sound like a person accepting a job.

107
00:07:15,711 --> 00:07:17,461
Yes. Thank you. Yes.

108
00:07:19,812 --> 00:07:22,773
Personnel conversion. Devotion.

109
00:07:25,872 --> 00:07:30,071
Purchase confirmed. Four
research associates.

110
00:07:33,341 --> 00:07:37,271
Nadia Okafor saw the manifest by
accident, in May.

111
00:07:37,841 --> 00:07:43,650
She had gone down to the tower's loading dock to
chase a delayed shipment of reagents,

112
00:07:43,670 --> 00:07:49,412
and the clerk, a young man called Deshawn who liked
her because she always said thank

113
00:07:49,432 --> 00:07:55,242
you, had handed her the wrong clipboard. It was a
manifest from a logistics company in

114
00:07:55,262 --> 00:08:01,411
Singapore called Ashford Pacific, for a delivery to an
Umbrella subsidiary in Nevada called

115
00:08:01,431 --> 00:08:02,495
Arklay Holdings.

116
00:08:03,067 --> 00:08:08,368
The cargo was listed as laboratory animals,
rhesus macaques, twenty-four.

117
00:08:08,937 --> 00:08:13,568
The delivery address was the old building on
the south bank. Sublevel A.

118
00:08:15,137 --> 00:08:20,815
There was no sublevel A. She had been in that
building for six years. She knew every room

119
00:08:20,835 --> 00:08:24,719
in it. There was a loading dock, a
basement, and four floors.

120
00:08:25,290 --> 00:08:31,032
And there was no licensed primate facility anywhere
in Umbrella Life Sciences. She knew

121
00:08:31,052 --> 00:08:36,794
that, too, because she was the director of virology,
and she would have had to sign for

122
00:08:36,814 --> 00:08:37,059
one.

123
00:08:38,429 --> 00:08:44,227
She gave the clipboard back to Deshawn, and thanked
him, and went back upstairs. But before

124
00:08:44,247 --> 00:08:50,363
she did, she took out her phone, and — with her heart
going faster than she liked — photographed

125
00:08:50,383 --> 00:08:51,194
the manifest.

126
00:08:51,764 --> 00:08:57,496
She did not know why. She told herself she would ask
about it. She never did. She kept

127
00:08:57,516 --> 00:09:03,182
the photograph in a folder on her phone called
Recipes, where no one would ever look.

128
00:09:05,251 --> 00:09:08,034
Catherine Adeyemi found the money in June.

129
00:09:08,605 --> 00:09:14,683
She found it the way good auditors find things — not
by looking for fraud, but by looking

130
00:09:14,703 --> 00:09:20,508
for symmetry, and noticing where it was missing.
The Arklay vault had cost twenty-two

131
00:09:20,528 --> 00:09:26,675
million dollars. She had seen it. She had stood in it.
But in the spring, Umbrella's Irish

132
00:09:26,695 --> 00:09:32,705
subsidiary had licensed intellectual property to
Ashford Pacific in Singapore for a fee,

133
00:09:32,725 --> 00:09:38,941
and Ashford Pacific had paid a Nevada company for
facility services, and the Nevada company

134
00:09:38,961 --> 00:09:44,834
had spent, over fourteen months, a hundred and forty
million dollars on something that

135
00:09:44,854 --> 00:09:50,727
had generated no construction invoices, no building
permits, no property taxes, and no

136
00:09:50,747 --> 00:09:51,618
depreciation.

137
00:09:52,187 --> 00:09:57,532
A hundred and forty million dollars had gone into
the ground, and nothing had come out.

138
00:09:59,101 --> 00:10:04,345
She went to his office at ten o'clock at night. He
was there. He was always there.

139
00:10:05,034 --> 00:10:09,170
I asked you for one thing that mattered. I asked
you to tell me where the money goes.

140
00:10:09,190 --> 00:10:12,054
You looked me in the eye and you took
me down to a freezer.

141
00:10:12,575 --> 00:10:13,771
The freezer is real.

142
00:10:14,291 --> 00:10:19,162
The freezer cost nineteen million. You've spent a
hundred and forty. Through three countries.

143
00:10:19,182 --> 00:10:23,633
On nothing. I want to know what the nothing is,
Albert, and I want to know it now, or

144
00:10:23,653 --> 00:10:28,262
I'm walking out of that door and taking my equity
with me and I'm going to be very, very

145
00:10:28,282 --> 00:10:31,103
interesting to the Securities and
Exchange Commission.

146
00:10:32,822 --> 00:10:34,711
He looked at her for a long time.

147
00:10:35,282 --> 00:10:41,060
The Cold-Read showed him everything, and it was not
what he had expected. She was angry,

148
00:10:41,080 --> 00:10:46,661
and she was frightened — but beneath the anger,
beneath the fear, there was something

149
00:10:46,681 --> 00:10:51,933
else. It was not a desire to leave. It was a
desire to be told a reason to stay.

150
00:10:52,503 --> 00:10:56,885
So he gave her one. He gave her,
precisely, one third of the truth.

151
00:10:58,375 --> 00:11:02,833
There is a secure research facility. It's under the
old building. It's better equipped

152
00:11:02,853 --> 00:11:07,363
than anything the government has, and it's working
on things that would terrify the FDA

153
00:11:07,383 --> 00:11:11,893
and — one day — save more lives than Aeterna ever
will. It isn't on any drawing because

154
00:11:11,913 --> 00:11:16,319
the people who draw buildings can't be trusted with
what's in this one. It's legal in

155
00:11:16,339 --> 00:11:19,079
exactly the way that the Manhattan
Project was legal.

156
00:11:19,599 --> 00:11:22,223
The Manhattan Project was run by
a government.

157
00:11:22,743 --> 00:11:25,232
Yes. And look how carefully they
spent the money.

158
00:11:26,751 --> 00:11:28,331
Is anyone being hurt?

159
00:11:28,851 --> 00:11:29,471
No.

160
00:11:30,792 --> 00:11:36,385
That was true, on the tenth of June, twenty
twenty-eight. He was careful to notice that

161
00:11:36,405 --> 00:11:40,515
it was true, and to say it in a way that
made it feel permanent.

162
00:11:42,203 --> 00:11:44,146
If you lie to me again, I'll know.

163
00:11:44,667 --> 00:11:47,624
You'll know I'm lying. You won't
always know which part.

164
00:11:48,144 --> 00:11:49,978
Is that supposed to reassure me?

165
00:11:50,498 --> 00:11:53,593
It's supposed to be honest. You
asked me for that, too.

166
00:11:55,613 --> 00:11:56,604
She stayed.

167
00:11:57,173 --> 00:12:02,569
She would tell herself, for years, that she stayed
because of the equity, which was by

168
00:12:02,589 --> 00:12:08,110
then worth two hundred million dollars. She would
tell herself she stayed to keep an eye

169
00:12:08,130 --> 00:12:13,463
on him — that someone sane should be in the room.
She would tell herself a great many

170
00:12:13,483 --> 00:12:13,903
things.

171
00:12:14,474 --> 00:12:20,220
The truth, which she only admitted to herself much
later, on a very bad night, was simpler.

172
00:12:20,240 --> 00:12:25,986
She stayed because it was the most interesting thing
that had ever happened to her. Because

173
00:12:26,006 --> 00:12:31,371
he was winning, and she had spent her whole career
watching men like him win, and she

174
00:12:31,391 --> 00:12:34,540
had wanted, just once, to be on
the side that did.

175
00:12:35,110 --> 00:12:41,123
That is how it happens. Not with a signature in
blood. With a sensible woman, alone in

176
00:12:41,143 --> 00:12:45,753
a glass office at midnight, deciding not
to ask the next question.

177
00:12:47,942 --> 00:12:49,145
Replace her.

178
00:12:49,666 --> 00:12:50,286
No.

179
00:12:50,806 --> 00:12:56,753
Buy a second Pell. A comptroller who cannot ask
questions. A woman who asks where the

180
00:12:56,773 --> 00:13:00,194
money goes will one day ask where
the bodies are.

181
00:13:00,714 --> 00:13:01,877
There are no bodies.

182
00:13:03,398 --> 00:13:04,210
Yet.

183
00:13:06,230 --> 00:13:12,380
Wesker did not answer that. He drove. And for the
first time, the silence in the car was

184
00:13:12,400 --> 00:13:15,886
not the silence of a man who had
won the argument.

185
00:13:17,956 --> 00:13:23,167
That night, in the cold room at the end of the white
corridor, Evelyn Marsh sat on the

186
00:13:23,187 --> 00:13:28,457
floor in front of the viewing window with her knees
drawn up, and talked to the Tyrant.

187
00:13:29,027 --> 00:13:34,494
Nobody heard what she said. The four research
associates the Director had bought for her

188
00:13:34,514 --> 00:13:39,857
that evening worked on in the laboratories without
looking up. They did not get tired,

189
00:13:39,877 --> 00:13:45,220
and they did not go home, and they did not find it
strange that their new director was

190
00:13:45,240 --> 00:13:50,520
sitting on the floor of the cold room at three in
the morning, speaking softly to the

191
00:13:50,540 --> 00:13:50,895
glass.

192
00:13:51,465 --> 00:13:57,267
After a while she stopped talking. She put her palm
flat against the window, exactly the

193
00:13:57,287 --> 00:14:01,634
way the Director had done in the basement,
eighteen months before.

194
00:14:02,205 --> 00:14:06,754
Inside, in the pale mist, the sleeping
thing did not open its eyes.

195
00:14:07,322 --> 00:14:13,871
But its hand, hanging at its side — its enormous grey
hand — moved. Just once. The fingers

196
00:14:13,891 --> 00:14:19,417
curled, and uncurled, the way a sleeping
child's hand closes around nothing.

197
00:14:19,988 --> 00:14:26,368
Evelyn Marsh saw it. She did not tell anyone. She sat
there until the sun came up, a hundred

198
00:14:26,388 --> 00:14:31,168
feet above her, over a river she could no
longer see, and she smiled.
